Here's an example of an upvote/downvote system for a comment board:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Comment Board with Votes</title>
    <style>
        .comments-container {
            max-width: 800px;
            margin: 0 auto;
            padding: 20px;
        }

        .comment {
            margin-bottom: 20px;
            padding: 15px;
            border: 1px solid #ddd;
            border-radius: 5px;
        }

        .vote-buttons {
            display: flex;
            margin-top: 10px;
            gap: 5px;
        }

        .vote-button {
            padding: 5px 10px;
            cursor: pointer;
            border: none;
            background: none;
            color: #666;
            font-size: 14px;
        }

        .vote-button:hover {
            color: #999;
        }

        .vote-count {
            margin-left: 10px;
            font-weight: bold;
        }

        .user-avatar {
            width: 40px;
            height: 40px;
            border-radius: 50%;
            margin-right: 10px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="comments-container">
        <div class="comment">
            <img src="https://gravatar.com/avatar/unknown" alt="User" class="user-avatar">
            <div class="vote-buttons">
                <span class="vote-button" onclick="vote('1', 'up')">↑</span>
                <span class="vote-count">0</span>
                <span class="vote-button" onclick="vote('1', 'down')">↓</span>
            </div>
            <div class="vote-count-total">Total: 0</div>
            <p>Comment text goes here...</p>
            <span class="timestamp">Posted by User on April 1, 2023</span>
        </div>
    </div>

    <script>
        function vote(commentId, type) {
            // This function would typically call a server-side script to record the vote
            // In a real application, you would send an AJAX POST request to /vote with commentId and type
            const updateCount = document.getElementById(`vote-count-${commentId}`);
            const total = document.getElementById(`vote-count-total-${commentId}`);

            if (type === 'up') {
                updateCount.textContent = parseInt(updateCount.textContent) + 1;
                total.textContent = parseInt(total.textContent) + 1;
                document.querySelector(`.vote-button[onclick="vote('${commentId}', 'down')"`).style.color = '#ff4444';
                document.querySelector(`.vote-button[onclick="vote('${commentId}', 'up')"`).style.color = '#44ff44';
            } else {
                updateCount.textContent = parseInt(updateCount.textContent) - 1;
                total.textContent = parseInt(total.textContent) - 1;
                document.querySelector(`.vote-button[onclick="vote('${commentId}', 'up')"`).style.color = '#8888ff';
                document.querySelector(`.vote-button[onclick="vote('${commentId}', 'down')"`).style.color = '#ff4444';
            }
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This code creates a simple upvote/downvote system with the following features:

1. Each comment can be voted on separately.
2. Votes are displayed with an up and down button.
3. The vote count updates in real-time.
4. The buttons change color after voting to indicate the direction of the vote.
5. Responsive design that works on different screen sizes.
6. Each comment includes a user avatar, timestamp, and total votes count.

To use this system:

1. When a user clicks an upvote button, it increments the upvote count and changes the button color to green.
2. When a user clicks a downvote button, it decrements the downvote count and changes the button color to red.
3. The total votes count updates to reflect the sum of up and down votes.

Note: This is a client-side implementation and the votes won't persist across page reloads. For a production environment, you would need to implement server-side vote counting and storage using technologies like PHP, Node.js, or other backend platforms.

To make it persist, you would need to add server-side storage, such as:
- Using PHP and MySQL
- Node.js with MongoDB
- Firebase
- Or any other web application framework

To use Gravatar for user avatars, replace the placeholder URL with the actual Gravatar URL for the user's email.

