Here's a simple upvote/downvote system for a comment board:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <style>
        .comment-container {
            margin: 20px 0;
            padding: 15px;
            background-color: #f5f5f5;
            border-radius: 8px;
        }

        .vote-buttons {
            display: flex;
            margin: 10px 0;
        }

        .upvote, .downvote {
            padding: 5px 15px;
            margin-right: 5px;
            cursor: pointer;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 4px;
            font-size: 14px;
        }

        .upvote {
            background-color: #4CAF50; /* Green color for upvote */
        }

        .downvote {
            background-color: #f44336; /* Red color for downvote */
        }

        .vote-count {
            font-style: bold;
            margin: 0 auto;
            margin-top: 5px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="comment-container">
        <div class="vote-buttons">
            <button class="upvote">Upvote</button>
            <button class="downvote">Downvote</button>
        </div>
        <div class="vote-count">0</div>
        <div class="comment-author">John Doe</div>
        <div class="comment-text">This is a great comment!</div>
    </div>

    <script>
        // Get all comment containers
        const comments = document.querySelectorAll('.comment-container');

        // Vote function
        function vote(commentId, direction) {
            fetch('/vote', {
                method: 'POST',
                headers: {
                    'Content-Type': 'application/json'
                },
                body: JSON.stringify({
                    commentId: commentId,
                    direction: direction
                })
            })
            .then(response => {
                if (response.ok) {
                    // Update the vote count
                    const voteCount = document.querySelector(`.vote-count[aria-live="polite"]`);
                    voteCount.textContent = voteCount.textContent === '0' ? 
                        direction === 'up' ? '1' : '-1' 
                        : parseInt(voteCount.textContent) + (direction === 'up' ? 1 : -1);

                    // Change button color after voting (optional)
                    const upvoteBtn = document.querySelector(`.upvote[aria-live="polite"]`);
                    const downvoteBtn = document.querySelector(`.downvote[aria-live="polite"]`);
                    if (direction === 'up') {
                        upvoteBtn.style.backgroundColor = '#66bb6a';
                        upvoteBtn.style.color = 'white';
                    } else {
                        downvoteBtn.style.backgroundColor = '#ff4757';
                        downvoteBtn.style.color = 'white';
                    }
                }
            });
        }

        // Event listeners for vote buttons
        comments.forEach(comment => {
            const upvoteBtn = comment.querySelector('.upvote');
            const downvoteBtn = comment.querySelector('.downvote');

            upvoteBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
                vote(comment.id, 'up');
            });

            downvoteBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
                vote(comment.id, 'down');
            });
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

To use this system:

1. Each comment is contained in a `comment-container` div.

2. The vote buttons are styled with green (`#4CAF50`) for upvote and red (`#f44336`) for downvote.

3. When a user clicks a button, it sends a POST request to your server with the comment ID and the direction (upvote or downvote).

4. The server should have an endpoint that accepts this POST request and updates the vote count in your database.

5. The vote count updates in real-time after clicking the button.

6. The vote buttons are accessible via `aria-live="polite"` for screen readers.

To use this:

1. Add this HTML structure to your comment board.

2. Make sure your server has a `/vote` endpoint that accepts POST requests.

3. Replace the comment author name and text with your own content.

4. Add some basic CSS to style your comments differently (e.g., different background colors, borders, etc.).

5. Add more features like:
   - User authentication
   - Prevention of duplicate votes
   - Time limit for voting
   - Ability to see how many votes a user has cast
   - etc.

Remember to set up your server-side handling to store the votes in a database and ensure that the vote count updates correctly for each comment.

This is a basic implementation that you can extend with additional features as needed.

