To create a data visualization showing COVID-19 cases in all U.S. states over the last two weeks, you can use tools like **Tableau**, **Power BI**, or **Python** with libraries like **Plotly** or **Matplotlib**. Below, I'll provide an example using **ECharts**, a popular JavaScript library for data visualization.

### Steps to Create the Visualization:

1. **Get the Data:**
   - Go to the [CDC COVID-19 Data Tracker](https://www.cdc.gov/coronavirus/cases/map/index.htm) or a reliable source like the [New York Times COVID-19 Data](https://github.com/nytimes/covid-19-data/tree/master/vaccine-data) to get the latest case numbers for each U.S. state.
   - Extract the data for the last two weeks (e.g., from July 1, 2024, to July 15, 2024).

2. **Clean and Prep the Data:**
   - Format the data in a tabular form, ensuring each row represents a state and each column represents a date.
   - Ensure the data is clean and accurate, checking for any missing values or inconsistencies.

3. **Create the Visualization:**
   - Use ECharts to create a line chart where each line represents a U.S. state.
   - Example code:

   ```javascript
   import echarts from 'echarts';
   import React from 'react';

   // Sample data (replace with your actual data)
   const data = [
     {
       state: 'State A',
       July 1: 100,
       July 2: 120,
       July 3: 150,
       July 4: 90,
       July 5: 200,
       July 6: 130,
       July 7: 175,
       July 8: 150,
       July 9: 180,
       July 10: 200,
       July 11: 190,
       July 12: 200,
       July 13: 220,
       July 14: 240,
       July 15: 250
     },
     // Repeat for other states...
   ];

   function createChart() {
     const chart = echarts.init(document.getElementById('main'));
     chart.setOption({
       title: {
         text: 'COVID-19 Cases in U.S. States (Last Two Weeks)'
       },
       tooltip: {
         formatter: function (value) {
           return new echarts.ToolTip({
             content: function (params) {
               return {
                 value: params.value,
                 title: params.seriesName
               };
             }
           });
       }
       },
       xAxis: {
         data: ['July 1', 'July 2', 'July 3', 'July 4', 'July 5', 'July 6', 'July 7', 'July 8', 'July 9', 'July 10', 'July 11', 'July 12', 'July 13', 'July 14', 'July 15'],
         },
       yAxis: {
         title: 'Number of Cases (Thousands)'
       },
       series: [
         {
           name: 'State A',
           data: data.StateA,
           type: 'line',
           smooth: true,
           color: '#FF0000'
         },
         {
           name: 'State B',
           data: data.StateB,
           type: 'line',
           smooth: true,
           color: '#00FFFF'
         },
         // Add lines for other states...
       ]
     });
   }

   createChart();
   ```

4. **Customize the Visualization:**
   - Adjust the styling (colors, fonts, etc.) to make it visually appealing.
   - Add a legend, title, and axis labels for better clarity.

5. **Host the Visualization:**
   - If you're using React, you can integrate this into a React component.
   - If you're using a static HTML page, save the code in an HTML file and open it in a browser.

6. **Interactivity:**
   - Use the chart's interactive features, such as hovering over data points to see exact numbers.

